+mode for a Slayer game only, and one it does not know as none. Then version 38
+lets the host's shield effects be `players` (SHIELD EFFECTS' PLAYERS' OWN), a
+value of distributed message 91 that tells each client to draw its own
+settings', not synced. Then version 39 marks a dedicated server's game (a
+host with no player of its own, upstream's `halo --server`, which the port
+leaves out) with a flag of its LAN advertisement (`HALO_PORT_ADVERTISED_DEDICATED_FLAG`,
+0x04 of the third reserved byte) and of its internet listing (32). With no
+new version, upstream's clients then tell the host their build (distributed
+message 92, `_distributed_message_client_build`), which the port does not
+send and, as a host, drops as the text chat's.
+Distributed message 93 (`network_damage.c`) needs no
+new version, as a host that does not know it drops it: a client whose
+players' shots leave from elsewhere than their units' cameras (a VR build,
+whose weapon fires from the player's hand) sends the host, unreliably every
+tick, where from: an offset from the camera of no more than 0.4 world units
+(a player leaning out and an arm). The host's line of sight check of a
+reported hit then also looks from there, as it does from a vehicle's gun,
+with the line from the player's eye to it clear, so that a hand past a
+corner the camera is behind still hits and a hand through a wall does not.
+Then version 40 adds to the join request (`message_client_join_game_request`,
+0x70 bytes before) upstream's Discord account's pass, 170 bytes, and its
+proof, 64, all zero for none: upstream's dedicated servers refuse a machine
+from a VPN's, Tor exit's or datacenter's address unless it shows one, with
+one of five new rejection codes. The port links no account, sends zeros,
+and as a host does not read them; it shows those codes, and the word
+filter's before them, as the game being closed.
+Then version 41 changes both vote messages' entries (`network_votekick.c`:
+the request's first byte, once the player's index, is the vote's kind; the
+status's first byte, once the player's index, is the kind, and it carries
+the no votes), adds `_rejection_code_address_full` (a dedicated server's
+`server.max_machines_per_address`), and upstream's dedicated server's map
+vote (`_distributed_message_map_vote`, 94, and `_map_vote_choice`, 95),
+which the port leaves out and drops as kinds it does not know. A co-op
+game's flags also tell its clients whether the host is a dedicated server
+(`_network_game_cooperative_dedicated_bit`: its loading zones then ask for
+most of the living players).
 
